    "body": "## Status\n\nThis is a proposal, not something I expect to be merged. The helper is large and opinionated, and the measured gains depend on the workload. It is posted for discussion and local experimentation.\n\nBuilding Nvim with PGO is a three-stage process: build an instrumented binary, run a training workload, then rebuild with the profile. It is easy to get wrong: the profile has to be next to the object files, ccache breaks instrumentation, and `-march` has to match between the stages. There is also no way to compare two builds, so the effect of a change cannot be checked.\n\nThis adds `contrib/pgo.sh`, which runs the three stages on the normal build system and can benchmark two binaries. It is opt-in and does not change the default build.\n\n## Changes\n\n- `contrib/pgo.sh`: `generate`, `train`, `use`, `all`, `bench`, and `clean` commands. GCC by default, `--clang` for Clang IR PGO. `--pgo-deps` also optimizes the bundled dependencies (GCC only). `--march` adds the same `-march` to both stages. ccache is disabled.\n- `contrib/pgo_train.lua`: editing workload (files, search, `:substitute`, treesitter, Lua callbacks, redraws, TUI). The functional test suite is run as well.\n- `contrib/pgo_bench.lua`: startup and micro-benchmarks. `bench` runs shuffled rounds from one shared path and reports the median ratio with its IQR, plus a same-binary control. `--suite` uses `test/benchmark` instead.\n- `DEPS_EXTRA_CFLAGS`: opt-in cache variable for the bundled dependencies, empty by default. `luv` appends it to its own `CMAKE_C_FLAGS`.\n- `BUILD.md`: documents the flow, the measured result, and what did not help.\n\n## Measured result\n\nAMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395 (Zen 5, GCC 16), both builds from the same sources and linking the same dependencies. Ratio = PGO / plain, so < 1.0 is faster; the range is the IQR over 21 shuffled rounds:\n\n| benchmark | ratio | benchmark | ratio |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| `regex_search` | 0.78 [0.77, 0.79] | `json_encode` | 0.98 [0.97, 1.00] |\n| `fn_substitute` | 0.81 [0.79, 0.82] | `startup` | 0.98 [0.98, 0.99] |\n| `api_getlines` | 0.93 [0.91, 0.95] | `fn_sort` | 0.98 [0.94, 1.02] |\n| `edit_files` | 0.96 [0.95, 0.98] | `json_decode` | 0.99 [0.93, 1.03] |\n| `option_get_set` | 0.98 [0.96, 0.99] | `treesitter_parse` | 1.00 [0.99, 1.00] |\n| `api_setlines` | 0.98 [0.97, 0.99] | | |\n\nThe same-binary control stays within [0.96, 1.02], so `fn_sort`, `json_decode`, and `treesitter_parse` are not measurable changes. Whole-process counters: instructions 0.92, branches 0.90, branch-misses 0.93, cycles 0.87.\n\n`--pgo-deps` makes the bundled tree-sitter parsers about 1.6x faster, but roughly doubles their build time. The default PGO build takes about 4 minutes on the test machine (32 threads) against about 25 seconds for a plain build with the dependencies already built; the training run is about 3 minutes of that.\n\n## Notes\n\n- `-march=native`, `-march=x86-64-v3`, Clang CS-PGO, BOLT, `-fno-plt`, `-fno-stack-protector`, `--gc-sections`, a larger training workload, and PGO on LuaJIT itself did not help; BUILD.md records the details.\n- The default PGO path does not need a build-system change. `DEPS_EXTRA_CFLAGS` is only used by `--pgo-deps` and is empty by default.\n- `contrib/pgo.sh` requires a Unix-like system; the instrumented binary has to run, so cross-compilation and Windows are not supported.\n\nAI-assisted\n",
